      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that the funds are released only when the agreed-upon terms are fulfilled. You'd be surprised, but it's a bit like a protective shield, guarding your money from any potential pitfalls, like a mischievous band of goblins guarding a treasure trove. \n\nIn Toronto, where the real estate market can be quite... lively, an escrow account is essential to protect both the buyer and the seller. Think of it as a temporary holding cell for your funds, where they're kept safe until the closing date. \n\nHere's how it typically works: your realtor will set up an escrow account, which will hold a portion of the purchase price until the closing date. This way, you can ensure that the funds are released only when the transaction is complete, and all conditions are met. It's a bit like a goblin's trap, where the funds are released only when the conditions are right, and the goblins (or in this case, the escrow agent) are satisfied.\n\nFor example, let's say you're buying a house in Toronto, and the seller is",
